          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Form the lowest possible 5-card hand. Lowest hand at showdown wins the pot.',
          gameplay: '1. **Ante**: All players post an ante. 2. **First Deal**: Each player receives 1 card face down and 1 card face up. 3. **Bring-in**: Player with the HIGHEST upcard (Ace counting as low) posts a compulsory bring-in bet. This is unique to lowballâ€”high card acts first and must bet. 4. **Subsequent Streets**: Three more cards dealt face up, with betting after each. 5. **Betting Order**: After the first round, betting begins with the player showing the LOWEST hand (pairs count as high). 6. **Showdown**: Best low hand wins.',
          keyRules: [
            'Lowest hand wins (ace-to-five ranking).',
            'Aces are always low (best card in the deck).',
            'Straights and flushes do NOT count against the player.',
            'A-2-3-4-5 (Wheel) is the best possible hand.',
            'First betting round: Highest upcard must bring-in (Ace is low, so King is worst).',
            'Subsequent rounds: Lowest hand showing acts first.',
            'Pairs are badâ€”you want five unpaired cards as low as possible.',
            'Compare highest cards first when breaking ties (7-5-4-3-2 beats 7-6-3-2-A because 5 < 6).',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Starting Hand Selection: Only play if your first two cards are both 8 or lower with no pair. If your hole card is paired with your upcard, or if you show a high card like a King or Queen, strongly consider folding.',
          intermediate: "Board Reading: Pay close attention to your opponents' upcards. If you hold A-3 in the hole but three other Aces are showing, your hand is much weaker. Conversely, if many low cards are showing, your medium-low hand gains value.",
          advanced: "Position Play: In later betting rounds, acting first with a strong low hand can be disadvantageous (you can't gain information). However, with a marginal hand in late position, you can make better decisions based on opponents' actions. The bring-in position (highest card showing) is at a significant disadvantage.",
          tips: [
            "The Ace is your best friendâ€”it's the lowest card in the deck.",
            'Avoid pairs at all costs. A hand with a pair of 2s loses to any no-pair hand, even one with a King.',
            "Watch for 'counterfeited' cards. If you have 7-5-4-3-2 and a 7 is dealt to an opponent, you're now playing 5-4-3-2-X.",
            "Don't chase with 9-low or worse if there's significant actionâ€”someone likely has you beat.",
            'The wheel (A-2-3-4-5) is unbeatableâ€”play it aggressively to build the pot.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
